WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:01.949
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:09.210
On the 6th December 1966 four guys from Liverpool
stepped into Abbey Road Studios and began

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.500
to record an album.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:18.180
333 hours and many questionable substances
later, The Beatles had emerged having produced

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.970
their eight album, Sgt.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.480
Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:29.360
It would go on to sell over 32 million copies
worldwide and be named the greatest album

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.650
of all time by Rolling Stone Magazine and
many other publications.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:41.470
It was highly experimental, using mould-breaking
techniques and a huge array of unusual instruments.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:47.760
The band had produced an emotional masterpiece
that epitomised the so called summer of love

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:55.040
and was a true masterpiece of its time, yet
it remains just as relevant and powerful today.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:02.890
Fast forward 44 years to 2010 and Justin Bieber
released his hit single "Baby", this is generally

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.420
considered to be a bad move.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.909
So what went wrong?

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:13.350
How did we go from Bob Dylan to Britney Spears,
from Led Zeppelin to Lady Gaga and The Kinks

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.560
to Katy Perry.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.490
But who am I to criticise the musical tastes
of the vast majority of today's youth?

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:24.900
Personally, my musical tastes are stuck in
middle of last century, but you may think

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.569
that just makes me old fashioned, stuck in
the past and I should move with the times.

00:01:30.569 --> 00:01:34.899
But here's the thing, there is far to this
than simple nostalgia and when your parents

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:41.030
keep telling you that the music died long
ago, they may actually have a point, because

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.619
it turns out science agrees with them.

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:49.270
Over the past thirty-plus years researchers
have been studying how trends in music have

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.420
changed.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.229
And a recent study in 2012 by the Spanish
National Research Council revealed that the

00:01:55.229 --> 00:02:03.209
suspicions of somewhat antiquated individuals
such as myself are very true, music IS getting

00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:05.700
worse every year.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.860
The researchers took around 500,000 recordings
from all genres of music from the period of

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:19.420
1955 to 2010 and they meticulously ran every
single song through a set of complex algorithms.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:26.130
These algorithms measured three distinct metrics
of each song, the harmonic complexity, timbral

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.100
diversity and loudness.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.720
The most shocking result that the researchers
found was that over the past few decades,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.250
timbre in songs has dropped drastically.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:44.810
Timbre is the texture, colour and quality
of the sounds within the music, in other words,

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.780
timbre is the song's richness and depth of
sound.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:55.620
The researchers found that timbral variety
peaked in the 1960s and has since been steadily

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.210
declining.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:02.810
The timbral palette has been homogenised,
meaning songs increasingly have less diversity

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.540
with their instruments and recording techniques.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:10.140
This divide is clearly evident if we take
what is widely considered to be The Beatle's

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:16.300
masterpiece, A Day In The Life, which was
recorded using an orchestra of forty musicians.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.950
But this is not classical music, this is pop.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:26.470
The five minute piece contains violins, violas,
cellos, double bass, a harp, clarinets, an

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:34.819
oboe, bassoons, flutes, french horns, trumpets,
trombones, a tuba and of course the four band

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:38.160
members playing their usual instruments over
the top.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:45.430
In contrast Robin Thicke's Blurred Lines uses
but one instrument, a drum machine.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.290
And yes this a rather extreme example, a song
known for it's one-dimensional but punchy

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.290
baseline.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:57.100
But it represents an overall trend with modern
pop music that the researchers found in their

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.100
data.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:02.490
Instead of experimenting with different musical
techniques and instruments, the vast majority

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:09.380
of pop today is built using the exact same
combination of a keyboard, drum machine, sampler

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.600
and computer software.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.299
This might be considered as progressive by
some, but in truth it sucks the creativity

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:22.880
and originality out of music, making everything
sound somewhat similar.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:29.009
Do you ever flick through the radio and think
to yourself "all these songs sound the same?".

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:33.569
What the researchers found is that the melodies,
rhythms and even the vocals of popular music

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:38.180
have become more and more similar to each
other since the sixties.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:43.639
One facet of this homogenisation of popular
music was pointed out by musical blogger Patrick

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:45.419
Metzger.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:50.979
Metzger noticed that hundreds of pop artists
were using the exact same sequence of notes

00:04:50.979 --> 00:04:57.550
that alternate between the fifth and third
notes of a major scale.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:03.500
This is usually accompanied by a vocal "Wa-oh-wa-oh"
pattern.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:13.100
Metzger named this the "Millennial Whoop"
and it sounds like this.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.960
The Millennial Whoop can be found in hundreds
of chart-topping pop songs created over the

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:23.080
past few years, and its usage is becoming
more frequent.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:29.199
From Katy Perry's California Girls to Justin
Bieber's baby, literally every single major

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:35.849
pop star today has included the Millennial
Whoop in at least one of their songs.

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:37.729
But why?

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:41.389
Well, quite simply, familiarity.

00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:46.490
Our brain likes familiarity, the more we hear
the same sounds the more we enjoy them.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:50.809
The millennial whoop has become a powerful
and predictable way to subconsciously say

00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:56.449
to the masses, "hey listen to this new song,
it's really cool, but don't worry you will

00:05:56.449 --> 00:06:03.139
like it because it's really familiar, you've
kind of heard it a hundred times before".

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:09.039
And in this wildly unpredictable world, this
makes us feel safe.

00:06:09.039 --> 00:06:14.789
Sticking to the same cookie-cutter formula
comforts people and that's important.

00:06:14.789 --> 00:06:16.839
But what about lyrics?

00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:19.689
Well, I'm afraid it's bad news there too.

00:06:19.689 --> 00:06:25.280
Another study examined the so called "Lyric
Intelligence" of hundreds of Billboard chart-topping

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.259
songs over the past ten years.

00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:33.569
They used different metrics such as the Flesch–Kincaid
readability index, which indicates how difficult

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:37.639
a piece of text is to understand and the quality
of the writing.

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:44.120
This was the result, over the past ten years
the average lyric intelligence has dropped

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.600
by a full grade.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.789
Lyrics are also getting shorter and tend to
repeat the same words more often.

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:58.279
We've gone from the absolute poetic beauty
of Bob Dylan and Morrissey too well... this...

00:06:58.279 --> 00:06:59.279
and this...

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:05.599
What if I also told you that the vast majority
of chart-topping music in the past 20 years

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:10.830
was written by just two people.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:17.669
What do Britney Spears, Taylor Swift, Ellie
Goulding, Robin Thicke, Jessie J, Justin Bieber,

00:07:17.669 --> 00:07:24.629
Katy Perry, Ariana Grande, Justin Timberlake,
Maroon 5, Pink, Leona Lewis, Avril Lavigne,

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:32.759
Christina Aguilera, Kesha, The Backstreet
Boys, Westlife, NSYNC, Adam Lambert and Will.i.am

00:07:32.759 --> 00:07:34.909
all have in common?

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:38.119
The answer: their songwriter.

00:07:38.119 --> 00:07:43.800
I'm not saying 100% of their songs, but a
good chunk of all of these artist's songs

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:50.000
were written by the same Swedish man, Mr.
Max Martin.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:57.229
This one man is singlehandedly responsible
for over two-dozen number one singles and

00:07:57.229 --> 00:08:01.539
thousands of songs in the top 100 charts over
the past decades.

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:07.080
He has written universally recognisable tracks
such as "I kissed a girl", "Baby one more

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:13.729
time", "Since u been gone", "California Gurls",
"Shake it off" and so, so many more.

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:20.629
And if Max Martin didn't write it American
signer-songwriter Lukasz Gottwald most probably

00:08:20.629 --> 00:08:21.629
did.

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:26.539
Known professionally as "Dr. Luke", together
with Max Martin, they account for the lyrics

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:31.559
and melodies behind the vast majority of pop
music today.

00:08:31.559 --> 00:08:35.680
You've likely never heard of them and that
is very intentional.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.140
These two men are the hidden pop factories
behind virtually every single band that is

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:46.040
played on the radio today and probably every
music act you grew up with, if you're under

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.250
thirty-years old.

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:51.830
And you wondered why everything sounds the
same.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:57.560
There are still popular, chart-topping musicians
that write the entirety of their own music

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:01.300
today, but you have to look really, really
hard.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:06.450
Research has also shown that the hook, the
part of the song that really grabs us and

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:11.680
pulls us in, is occurring sooner in modern
songs and they happen more often.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:16.740
Researchers believe this is because when it
comes to music, our attention spans have drastically

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:22.600
shortened, unless a song instantly grabs us
our brains tend to shut off and ignore it,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.960
often skipping to the next song.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.740
This shortened attention span is a trend amongst
people that has only occurred in the past

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:35.960
ten years and it's believed to have been caused
by the instant access to millions of songs

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.370
at our fingertips.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:41.700
It used to be the case that if you wanted
to hear a song you had to go out and buy that

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.680
one single or album, take it home and play
it.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.150
You would probably play it countless times
because you had spent so much money on so

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.960
few songs.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.460
Over time you would learn to appreciate all
the subtle nuances throughout the album.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:02.790
And then the iPod happened granting access
to thousands of songs on one device, which

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.040
eventually led to streaming.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.060
Today we flick through songs on Spotify without
much thought to each song's subtleties and

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:11.860
unique talents.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:17.000
This has caused musicians and record companies
to favour punchy bass lines that demand our

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:23.200
attention and to stuff each song full of so
called "hooks" to instantly grab our attention

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:26.580
and keep it for as long as possible.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.880
And they've been doing something else in recent
years to grab our attention, something subtle

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.540
but very powerful, yet so very, very wrong.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:41.060
For the past twenty years music producers
have been engaged in a war.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.960
The "loudness war".

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.760
The aim of this war is to produce louder music
than your competitors.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.730
But how do you make music louder when the
listener is in control of the volume, not

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:54.030
the producer?

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.740
Well, they use compression.

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:01.360
You may have heard of dynamic range compression,
it's the process of boosting the volume of

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.100
the quietest parts of a song so they match
the loudest parts, thus reducing the dynamic

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:12.200
range, the distance between the loudest part
and quietest part.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:17.181
This makes the whole song sound much, much
louder than the un-compressed version, no

00:11:17.181 --> 00:11:21.310
matter what volume the listener has set their
device to.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:26.220
It's like me standing in the middle of the
street and mumbling nonsense to myself, occasionally

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.000
whispers and sometimes speaking a bit louder.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.330
A few people might notice and avoid me.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:36.700
But then if I were to compress my dynamic
range I would suddenly be bellowing out every

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:40.620
single word at the top of my voice, loudly
and proudly.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.410
Suddenly everyone turns around to look at
the crazy man shouting in the street and the

00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:46.710
police would be called.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:52.660
But this is exactly why producers do it, as
the market has become increasingly crammed

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:58.940
with similar sounding pop music, making your
song shout louder than all the others ensures

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:02.220
it will be heard amongst all the competition.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:06.560
But there's a big price to pay for loudness.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.480
Dynamic range compression, when abused, as
it often is today, is an absolute travesty

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.590
when it comes to the art of creating music.

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:20.550
Where physics is concerned, the rule is that
you can't make a sound louder than the volume

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:24.700
it was recorded at, without reducing its quality.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.890
Compressing a song's dynamic range strips
away its timbral variety.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:33.880
It muddies the sound, subtle nuances that
would have before been very noticeable and

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:40.220
could have been appreciated are now, no longer
nuanced, they sound exactly the same as the

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:42.160
rest of the track.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:13:01.610
Listen to this short recording without any
compression.

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:06.240
Now hear what happens when the dynamic range
is compressed to match that of modern pop

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:22.970
music.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:29.530
Hear how everything sounds less punchy and
vibrant, the drum beats stand out less, everything

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.780
just makes less of an impact.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:39.810
But there's very real reason why popular musicians
and producers today don't stray away from

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:46.070
their safe-haven of repetitive, monotonous
drum machines, unimaginative, factory-produced

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:51.300
lyrics, rhythms stolen then from the previous
popular song then chopped up and changed slightly

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:55.030
and of course, their ever popular millennial
whoops.

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:57.990
It all has to do with risk.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:03.820
In the fifties, sixties and seventies record
labels would receive hundreds of demo tapes

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.620
from budding young artists every week.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:11.280
They would sift through them and the most
talented acts would be offered record contracts.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.670
Even if they weren't that special it didn't
matter too much, the record label would just

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:20.610
through a few thousand pounds into marketing
and if the public liked their music they would

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:27.510
gain traction organically and make it big,
if not, they would fade away into the night.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:33.460
And this is crucial because importantly, the
public were voting with their ears for the

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:37.860
best, the most talented musicians, singers
and songwriters.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:43.050
We, the people were the final judge and jury,
the ultimate arbiter.

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:49.460
And so musicians had to be really bloody talented
to impress us enough to stick around and make

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:54.910
more music.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:15:00.630
But this was risky, because many times record
labels would pump thousands of pounds into

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:07.480
an act that weren't destined to be and their
gamble wouldn't pay off, losing their investment.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:12.070
But when they signed the really big acts it
would balance the books.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.750
However today promoting a new band is more
expensive than ever.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:23.570
Over time the cost of breaking in a new artist
onto the global music scene has sky-rocketed.

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:30.971
In fact the IFPI reports that today it costs
anywhere between $500,000 and $3,000,000 TO

00:15:30.971 --> 00:15:36.750
sign a new act and break them into the music
scene; that's a hell of a lot of money.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:39.100
Would you want to gamble with three million
dollars?

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:40.100
No?

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.779
Neither do music producers.

00:15:41.779 --> 00:15:46.160
So the industry has reacted by removing the
risk.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:52.180
Instead of trying to find genuine musical
talent they simply take a pretty young face,

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:58.290
usually from a TV talent show and then simply
force the public to like them, by brainwashing

00:15:58.290 --> 00:15:59.290
them.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:02.730
Instead of allowing the public to grow to
like an artist and make their own mind up

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:09.140
about the quality of their music, the industry
now simply makes you like the music, thus

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:12.270
removing all the financial risk.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.270
Brainwash you say?

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:15.010
How on earth do they do that?

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:21.580
Have you ever noticed how "that" popular new
song seems to follow you around, everywhere

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:22.880
you go.

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:29.340
It's on every radio station, it's played in
your favourite stores, the supermarket, online

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:34.210
and its even in the latest Hollywood movies
and popular TV shows?

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:36.860
This is no coincidence.

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:42.120
What that is in fact, is the record label's
$3 million making sure that that new single

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:51.370
is quite literally everywhere, completely
unescapable.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.900
Remember I was talking about the power of
familiarity?

00:16:53.900 --> 00:17:01.300
It's called the Mere-exposure effect, a physiological
phenomenon by which people develop a preference

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.400
for things they see and hear often.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:09.680
Our brain releases dopamine when we hear a
song we've heard a few times before and the

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.650
effect only gets stronger with each listen.

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:16.860
Can you remember the very first time you heard
your favourite pop songs from the past ten

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:17.860
years?

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:24.391
Whether it be Gangnam Style, Happy, All About
That Bass, Blurred Lines, Hotline Bling, did

00:17:24.391 --> 00:17:28.410
you truly like it the first time you heard
it?

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:31.280
Or where you kind of repulsed?

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:36.770
Did you have this brief moment where you thought,
what the hell is this?

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:42.400
But then you heard it a few more times and
you began to think, well I guess it's kinda

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:43.400
catchy.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:47.300
And they your friends are all listening to
it and you hear it a few times and boom, it's

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:50.559
your favourite song and you can't stop listening
to it.

00:17:50.559 --> 00:17:55.120
If this has happened to you then I'm afraid,
you have been brainwashed.

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:58.870
The mere-exposure effect has gotten to you.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:04.460
Surely if a song is truly a great song, then
you wouldn't need to force yourself to love

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:09.730
it, you wouldn't need to be won over through
a period of repeated exposure, you would just

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.140
like it the first time you heard it.

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:19.200
We all have different musical tastes but they
are sadly being overridden, diluted and emulsified

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:24.550
by the brainwashing activities of big record
labels, the repeated and constant exposure

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.950
to manufactured songs that we've heard a hundred
times before.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:36.000
Don't get me wrong, there are many fantastically
talented bands out there, but in today's industry

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:42.640
virtually none of them will ever be signed
because they are simply too risky to promote,

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:47.180
because they don't fit the usual pop formula...
they are different.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:51.309
But being different is important.

00:18:51.309 --> 00:18:56.530
You may be thinking, "so what if I'm being
brainwashed, I enjoy contemporary popular

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:59.200
music and isn't that what's important?"

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:04.790
Yes, of course, music is an expression of
your personality and it should be enjoyed,

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:07.800
no matter what others think.

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:14.610
But it's also really important to not let
creativity and originality disappear.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:20.580
Music as an art form is dying, it's being
replaced by music which is a disposable product,

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:23.500
designed to sell but not to inspire.

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:29.600
So we shouldn't be so complacent in allowing
systematic, cold, factory produced music to

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:36.020
dominate or else the beautiful, soulful and
truly real music that we've all at some point

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:42.650
loved and has been there through our darkest
times and our happiest times, could soon be

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:49.040
a distant memory, never to be repeated.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:20:00.169
Thanks for watching.

